        "id": 145230,
        "name": "arenson9",
        "username": "arenson9",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/arenson9/{size}/1900_2.png",
        "created_at": "2015-07-28T15:52:13.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"Trajector\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/trajector/48/1914_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Trajector:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<div><b>AlexxKay wrote:</b><br><blockquote>Arcanist Lupus wrote:\n&nbsp;\n<blockquote>\nMaybe it does have a stack, but not the same way MtG does.&nbsp; ... trying to understand SotM timing using Magic's stack will frequently provide the wrong answer, which is why we try to discourage people from saying that SotM has a stack. &nbsp;Maybe it does, but the comparison is rarely helpful.\n</blockquote>\nThank you for the patient and elucidating reply!I guess my objection is because I first had the term \"stack\" enter my brain about 30 years ago in a Computer Science class, and both MtG and SotM clearly (to me) follow *that* concept :-)\n</blockquote></div>\nDouble agree! I was also confused by people saying that Sentinels didn't have a stack, because I'm familiar with computational data structures but not with Magic. Now I know!\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>This</p>",
